$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 06/Practice
::HC-W6-P01::A vaccination leaflet lists eight scientific facts before mentioning where parents should take children. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=buried main message and action#Correct. Users should not have to search for the main point.
~jargon and vague action#Not the best answer. Users should not have to search for the main point.
~incomplete risk framing#Not the best answer. Users should not have to search for the main point.
~weak pre-test question#Not the best answer. Users should not have to search for the main point.
}

::HC-W6-P02::A vaccination leaflet lists eight scientific facts before mentioning where parents should take children. What is the strongest next response? {
=Put the main action and essential benefit early, then support it with only the detail needed for the decision.#Correct. Users should not have to search for the main point.
~Replace the phrase with a concrete instruction specifying what symptoms and when to return.#Not the best answer. Users should not have to search for the main point.
~Add absolute risk or natural frequencies so people can judge the size of the benefit.#Not the best answer. Users should not have to search for the main point.
~Ask what they think the message means, what action is expected, what is confusing and what feels credible.#Not the best answer. Users should not have to search for the main point.
}

::HC-W6-P03::A discharge sheet says present for clinical evaluation if symptoms persist. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=jargon and vague action#Correct. Actionability depends on understandable, specific direction.
~incomplete risk framing#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on understandable, specific direction.
~weak pre-test question#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on understandable, specific direction.
~failure of cultural and linguistic adaptation#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on understandable, specific direction.
}

::HC-W6-P04::A discharge sheet says present for clinical evaluation if symptoms persist. What is the strongest next response? {
=Replace the phrase with a concrete instruction specifying what symptoms and when to return.#Correct. Actionability depends on understandable, specific direction.
~Add absolute risk or natural frequencies so people can judge the size of the benefit.#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on understandable, specific direction.
~Ask what they think the message means, what action is expected, what is confusing and what feels credible.#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on understandable, specific direction.
~Adapt meaning and tone with local speakers, then pre-test the revised version.#Not the best answer. Actionability depends on understandable, specific direction.
}

::HC-W6-P05::A campaign states a treatment cuts risk by 50 percent but does not provide the baseline risk. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=incomplete risk framing#Correct. Relative effects alone can sound larger than the underlying change.
~weak pre-test question#Not the best answer. Relative effects alone can sound larger than the underlying change.
~failure of cultural and linguistic adaptation#Not the best answer. Relative effects alone can sound larger than the underlying change.
~competing actions#Not the best answer. Relative effects alone can sound larger than the underlying change.
}

::HC-W6-P06::A campaign states a treatment cuts risk by 50 percent but does not provide the baseline risk. What is the strongest next response? {
=Add absolute risk or natural frequencies so people can judge the size of the benefit.#Correct. Relative effects alone can sound larger than the underlying change.
~Ask what they think the message means, what action is expected, what is confusing and what feels credible.#Not the best answer. Relative effects alone can sound larger than the underlying change.
~Adapt meaning and tone with local speakers, then pre-test the revised version.#Not the best answer. Relative effects alone can sound larger than the underlying change.
~Prioritise the single most important action or clearly sequence actions by audience and context.#Not the best answer. Relative effects alone can sound larger than the underlying change.
}

::HC-W6-P07::Pre-test participants are asked only whether they like a poster. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=weak pre-test question#Correct. Preference does not establish comprehension or usability.
~failure of cultural and linguistic adaptation#Not the best answer. Preference does not establish comprehension or usability.
~competing actions#Not the best answer. Preference does not establish comprehension or usability.
~unrealistic testing context#Not the best answer. Preference does not establish comprehension or usability.
}

::HC-W6-P08::Pre-test participants are asked only whether they like a poster. What is the strongest next response? {
=Ask what they think the message means, what action is expected, what is confusing and what feels credible.#Correct. Preference does not establish comprehension or usability.
~Adapt meaning and tone with local speakers, then pre-test the revised version.#Not the best answer. Preference does not establish comprehension or usability.
~Prioritise the single most important action or clearly sequence actions by audience and context.#Not the best answer. Preference does not establish comprehension or usability.
~Test the material under conditions that resemble actual exposure and time constraints.#Not the best answer. Preference does not establish comprehension or usability.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 06/Mastery
::HC-W6-M01::A technically accurate English slogan is translated literally and becomes offensive in a local language. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=failure of cultural and linguistic adaptation#Correct. Literal translation can preserve words while damaging meaning.
~competing actions#Not the best answer. Literal translation can preserve words while damaging meaning.
~unrealistic testing context#Not the best answer. Literal translation can preserve words while damaging meaning.
~non-actionable benefit statement#Not the best answer. Literal translation can preserve words while damaging meaning.
}

::HC-W6-M02::A technically accurate English slogan is translated literally and becomes offensive in a local language. What is the strongest next response? {
=Adapt meaning and tone with local speakers, then pre-test the revised version.#Correct. Literal translation can preserve words while damaging meaning.
~Prioritise the single most important action or clearly sequence actions by audience and context.#Not the best answer. Literal translation can preserve words while damaging meaning.
~Test the material under conditions that resemble actual exposure and time constraints.#Not the best answer. Literal translation can preserve words while damaging meaning.
~Specify the feasible protective action, where to obtain what is needed and when to act.#Not the best answer. Literal translation can preserve words while damaging meaning.
}

::HC-W6-M03::A poster contains three competing calls to action: test, donate and attend a meeting. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=competing actions#Correct. Multiple equal calls to action dilute decision clarity.
~unrealistic testing context#Not the best answer. Multiple equal calls to action dilute decision clarity.
~non-actionable benefit statement#Not the best answer. Multiple equal calls to action dilute decision clarity.
~misleading visual encoding#Not the best answer. Multiple equal calls to action dilute decision clarity.
}

::HC-W6-M04::A poster contains three competing calls to action: test, donate and attend a meeting. What is the strongest next response? {
=Prioritise the single most important action or clearly sequence actions by audience and context.#Correct. Multiple equal calls to action dilute decision clarity.
~Test the material under conditions that resemble actual exposure and time constraints.#Not the best answer. Multiple equal calls to action dilute decision clarity.
~Specify the feasible protective action, where to obtain what is needed and when to act.#Not the best answer. Multiple equal calls to action dilute decision clarity.
~Use a simple proportional display with labelled axes and no decorative distortion.#Not the best answer. Multiple equal calls to action dilute decision clarity.
}

::HC-W6-M05::Programme staff approve a brochure after reading it in a quiet office, but clinic users see it while queuing for less than two minutes. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unrealistic testing context#Correct. Usability depends on the real context of use.
~non-actionable benefit statement#Not the best answer. Usability depends on the real context of use.
~misleading visual encoding#Not the best answer. Usability depends on the real context of use.
~need for behavioural usability testing#Not the best answer. Usability depends on the real context of use.
}

::HC-W6-M06::Programme staff approve a brochure after reading it in a quiet office, but clinic users see it while queuing for less than two minutes. What is the strongest next response? {
=Test the material under conditions that resemble actual exposure and time constraints.#Correct. Usability depends on the real context of use.
~Specify the feasible protective action, where to obtain what is needed and when to act.#Not the best answer. Usability depends on the real context of use.
~Use a simple proportional display with labelled axes and no decorative distortion.#Not the best answer. Usability depends on the real context of use.
~Observe users explaining or demonstrating dosing and revise layout or instructions based on errors.#Not the best answer. Usability depends on the real context of use.
}

::HC-W6-M07::A malaria message says protect your family without explaining how. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=non-actionable benefit statement#Correct. Motivation without operational instruction may not enable action.
~misleading visual encoding#Not the best answer. Motivation without operational instruction may not enable action.
~need for behavioural usability testing#Not the best answer. Motivation without operational instruction may not enable action.
~vague probability language#Not the best answer. Motivation without operational instruction may not enable action.
}

::HC-W6-M08::A malaria message says protect your family without explaining how. What is the strongest next response? {
=Specify the feasible protective action, where to obtain what is needed and when to act.#Correct. Motivation without operational instruction may not enable action.
~Use a simple proportional display with labelled axes and no decorative distortion.#Not the best answer. Motivation without operational instruction may not enable action.
~Observe users explaining or demonstrating dosing and revise layout or instructions based on errors.#Not the best answer. Motivation without operational instruction may not enable action.
~Use an understandable numeric frequency and compare it with relevant benefits or risks.#Not the best answer. Motivation without operational instruction may not enable action.
}

::HC-W6-M09::A chart uses 3D bars that make small differences appear large. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=misleading visual encoding#Correct. Visual design can alter perceived magnitude.
~need for behavioural usability testing#Not the best answer. Visual design can alter perceived magnitude.
~vague probability language#Not the best answer. Visual design can alter perceived magnitude.
~ceremonial pre-testing#Not the best answer. Visual design can alter perceived magnitude.
}

::HC-W6-M10::A chart uses 3D bars that make small differences appear large. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use a simple proportional display with labelled axes and no decorative distortion.#Correct. Visual design can alter perceived magnitude.
~Observe users explaining or demonstrating dosing and revise layout or instructions based on errors.#Not the best answer. Visual design can alter perceived magnitude.
~Use an understandable numeric frequency and compare it with relevant benefits or risks.#Not the best answer. Visual design can alter perceived magnitude.
~Pause production, document the issue and revise before scaling the material.#Not the best answer. Visual design can alter perceived magnitude.
}

::HC-W6-M11::A medicine label is rewritten in simpler language but dosing errors persist. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=need for behavioural usability testing#Correct. Readability alone does not guarantee correct use.
~vague probability language#Not the best answer. Readability alone does not guarantee correct use.
~ceremonial pre-testing#Not the best answer. Readability alone does not guarantee correct use.
~recall substituted for comprehension#Not the best answer. Readability alone does not guarantee correct use.
}

::HC-W6-M12::A medicine label is rewritten in simpler language but dosing errors persist. What is the strongest next response? {
=Observe users explaining or demonstrating dosing and revise layout or instructions based on errors.#Correct. Readability alone does not guarantee correct use.
~Use an understandable numeric frequency and compare it with relevant benefits or risks.#Not the best answer. Readability alone does not guarantee correct use.
~Pause production, document the issue and revise before scaling the material.#Not the best answer. Readability alone does not guarantee correct use.
~Measure whether people can explain the intended meaning and next action, not only repeat words.#Not the best answer. Readability alone does not guarantee correct use.
}

::HC-W6-M13::A message says vaccine side effects are extremely rare without defining the frequency. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=vague probability language#Correct. Words such as rare are interpreted differently by different people.
~ceremonial pre-testing#Not the best answer. Words such as rare are interpreted differently by different people.
~recall substituted for comprehension#Not the best answer. Words such as rare are interpreted differently by different people.
~inaccessible visual design#Not the best answer. Words such as rare are interpreted differently by different people.
}

::HC-W6-M14::A message says vaccine side effects are extremely rare without defining the frequency. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use an understandable numeric frequency and compare it with relevant benefits or risks.#Correct. Words such as rare are interpreted differently by different people.
~Pause production, document the issue and revise before scaling the material.#Not the best answer. Words such as rare are interpreted differently by different people.
~Measure whether people can explain the intended meaning and next action, not only repeat words.#Not the best answer. Words such as rare are interpreted differently by different people.
~Add labels, patterns or symbols and test contrast so meaning does not depend on colour perception.#Not the best answer. Words such as rare are interpreted differently by different people.
}

::HC-W6-M15::A message pre-test finds confusion but the final product is unchanged because printing is already booked. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=ceremonial pre-testing#Correct. Pre-testing has value only if findings can change the product.
~recall substituted for comprehension#Not the best answer. Pre-testing has value only if findings can change the product.
~inaccessible visual design#Not the best answer. Pre-testing has value only if findings can change the product.
~selection bias in message testing#Not the best answer. Pre-testing has value only if findings can change the product.
}

::HC-W6-M16::A message pre-test finds confusion but the final product is unchanged because printing is already booked. What is the strongest next response? {
=Pause production, document the issue and revise before scaling the material.#Correct. Pre-testing has value only if findings can change the product.
~Measure whether people can explain the intended meaning and next action, not only repeat words.#Not the best answer. Pre-testing has value only if findings can change the product.
~Add labels, patterns or symbols and test contrast so meaning does not depend on colour perception.#Not the best answer. Pre-testing has value only if findings can change the product.
~Include intended non-users and people facing the barriers the campaign aims to change.#Not the best answer. Pre-testing has value only if findings can change the product.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 06/Institutional
::HC-W6-I01::A campaign claims a message is understandable because 90 percent recalled the slogan. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=recall substituted for comprehension#Correct. Memory of a slogan can coexist with misunderstanding.
~inaccessible visual design#Not the best answer. Memory of a slogan can coexist with misunderstanding.
~selection bias in message testing#Not the best answer. Memory of a slogan can coexist with misunderstanding.
~buried main message and action#Not the best answer. Memory of a slogan can coexist with misunderstanding.
}

::HC-W6-I02::A campaign claims a message is understandable because 90 percent recalled the slogan. What is the strongest next response? {
=Measure whether people can explain the intended meaning and next action, not only repeat words.#Correct. Memory of a slogan can coexist with misunderstanding.
~Add labels, patterns or symbols and test contrast so meaning does not depend on colour perception.#Not the best answer. Memory of a slogan can coexist with misunderstanding.
~Include intended non-users and people facing the barriers the campaign aims to change.#Not the best answer. Memory of a slogan can coexist with misunderstanding.
~Put the main action and essential benefit early, then support it with only the detail needed for the decision.#Not the best answer. Memory of a slogan can coexist with misunderstanding.
}

::HC-W6-I03::A graphic uses red and green alone to distinguish safe and unsafe categories. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=inaccessible visual design#Correct. Accessibility requires redundant visual cues.
~selection bias in message testing#Not the best answer. Accessibility requires redundant visual cues.
~buried main message and action#Not the best answer. Accessibility requires redundant visual cues.
~jargon and vague action#Not the best answer. Accessibility requires redundant visual cues.
}

::HC-W6-I04::A graphic uses red and green alone to distinguish safe and unsafe categories. What is the strongest next response? {
=Add labels, patterns or symbols and test contrast so meaning does not depend on colour perception.#Correct. Accessibility requires redundant visual cues.
~Include intended non-users and people facing the barriers the campaign aims to change.#Not the best answer. Accessibility requires redundant visual cues.
~Put the main action and essential benefit early, then support it with only the detail needed for the decision.#Not the best answer. Accessibility requires redundant visual cues.
~Replace the phrase with a concrete instruction specifying what symptoms and when to return.#Not the best answer. Accessibility requires redundant visual cues.
}

::HC-W6-I05::A pre-test sample contains only people already using the service the message is meant to promote. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=selection bias in message testing#Correct. Existing users may interpret the product differently from priority audiences.
~buried main message and action#Not the best answer. Existing users may interpret the product differently from priority audiences.
~jargon and vague action#Not the best answer. Existing users may interpret the product differently from priority audiences.
~incomplete risk framing#Not the best answer. Existing users may interpret the product differently from priority audiences.
}

::HC-W6-I06::A pre-test sample contains only people already using the service the message is meant to promote. What is the strongest next response? {
=Include intended non-users and people facing the barriers the campaign aims to change.#Correct. Existing users may interpret the product differently from priority audiences.
~Put the main action and essential benefit early, then support it with only the detail needed for the decision.#Not the best answer. Existing users may interpret the product differently from priority audiences.
~Replace the phrase with a concrete instruction specifying what symptoms and when to return.#Not the best answer. Existing users may interpret the product differently from priority audiences.
~Add absolute risk or natural frequencies so people can judge the size of the benefit.#Not the best answer. Existing users may interpret the product differently from priority audiences.
}

